[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1852","stub":{"id":"1852","section":"01. Flaming Gorge to Lodore (CO) ","altname":"","river":"Green","difficulty":"ItoIIstandoutIII","states":["USA-COL","USA-UTA"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"above-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"6760","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"Head south on FR 219 off of hwy 191 between the Flaming Gorge Resevoir and Dutch John Utah. There is a day use kiosk taking payment for use of the boat ramp ajacent to parking and staging areas. The ramp is frequently very busy with commercial fly fishermen and their guests. A long, steep starcase connects the parking area with the launching area.","id":"110876","location":{"latitude":"40.909","longitude":"-109.422"},"name":"Spillway Boat Launch","distance":"0","photoID":"2rRRl3gx4e3GF8PUnttRG","difficulty":"I","approximate":false},{"type":"put-in","description":"The Little Hole National Recreation Trail follows the north side of the Green River between Flaming Gorge Dam and Little Hole seven miles down-stream. Hiking and fishing access are the major attractions. Camping, fires, and horses are NOT allowed on this section and mountain biking is NOT allowed Memorial Day to Labor Day weekends.\n\nThe lower Little Hole Trail extends from Little Hole about two miles downstream. Horses and moun- tain bikes are welcome. Float-in or hike-in camping is allowed only in designated riverside camps.","id":"YF19TfuuCXWUIXU4ljbIV","location":{"latitude":"40.910242","longitude":"-109.314809"},"name":"Little Hole","distance":"7","photoID":"LyZgsTrqulXQDP4Nf2GU4","difficulty":"II","approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"Red Creek Rapid is a class III rapid that can be vary greately depending on the flow of the river. At low flows there are two channels. The majority of the water goes right, with a series of holes towards the top, followed by two large bolders to avoid and a bolder garden in the exit. The left channel is shallower but straighter with a similar boulder garden below.","id":"Ls7u5AAuEVeyGkaHjSc8B","location":{"latitude":"40.903697","longitude":"-109.250488"},"name":"Red Creek Rapid","distance":"10.9","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"put-in","description":"(BLM) North side (river left) Fee Area. Includes boat ramp, drinking water, toilets, and dump station.\n\nMile 15.4","id":"104634","location":{"latitude":"40.89747619628906","longitude":"-109.18180084228516"},"name":"Indian Crossing Boat Launch","distance":"15.4","photoID":"0","difficulty":"II","approximate":false},{"type":"hazard","description":"Bridge is not passable at flows of 4500 CFS or greater (BLM Map Warning) as water is too high to pass under the bridge. Take out prior to bridge at Indian Crossing Campground or Bridge Hollow Campground.","id":"QE8q9LsfttCpi3Qj0aHSU","location":{"latitude":"40.899497","longitude":"-109.171853"},"name":"Taylor Flat Bridge","distance":"16.05","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"put-in","description":"Located 400 yards downstream from Indian Crossing is the Bridge Hollow Campground (fee area) which offers drinking water, toilets, boat ramps, and a credit card phone.","id":"wX0iCmk5Je6bb9X7CZrzq","location":{"latitude":"40.8998","longitude":"-109.17002"},"name":"Bridge Hollow Campground","distance":"16.15","photoID":null,"difficulty":"II","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"Gates of Lodore launch ramp. Free play permit from Dinosour National Monument required to end at this location","id":"116144","location":{"latitude":"40.727089","longitude":"-108.888565"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"47.42","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"7LocLiQfE0bz3a9UmFN2F","title":"Springtime release from Flaming Gorge","post_date":"2022-06-01 06:15:50+00","detail":"Recreationists on the Green River should take caution and be aware the increased flows in the river will be cold and swift. LTSP releases are scheduled include a ramp up to full powerplant releases and full bypass the following day, maintaining 8,600 cubic feet per second (cfs) for 7 days, then followed by a 2,000 cfs/day ramp down rate.\n\nForecasted (tentative) hourly release schedules can be found by visiting the Colorado River Basin Forecast Center at this website: https://www.cbrfc.noaa.gov/station/flowplot/flowplot.cgi?GRZU1. However, updates could take some time, a day or so, to be reflected on the website. Tabulated hourly data can be found on this page under the link, “Hourly Forecast Flow csv” in the lower right hand corner of the webpage, or navigating to this link: https://www.cbrfc.noaa.gov/product/hydrofcst/RVFCSV/GRZU1.fflw1.csv.","user":{"name":"Hattie Johnson"}},{"id":"101254986","title":null,"post_date":"2020-08-18 04:00:00+00","detail":"The Western Area Power Administration has alerted Reclamation to an increased possibility that Flaming Gorge Dam’s power plant will need to augment power supplies in the event of a potential power system emergency. As temperatures across the Southwest US remain high and/or increase over the next week or so, the potential for this need increases.\n\nThe Bureau of Reclamation urges those recreating on or along the Green River below Flaming Gorge to exercise increased caution as possible sudden fluctuations in water releases to meet summer energy demands may cause rapid changes to the river’s flow. The Bureau of Reclamation advises those recreating along the river—including river runners, anglers, campers and hikers—to be vigilant and exercise increased caution through the summer months. Unscheduled water release changes may occur without notice. Vessels should be secured to withstand potential changes in water levels and campers should avoid setting camp where a sudden river rise could reach tents or other camping supplies.","user":{"name":"Hattie Johnson"}}],"p